What do you think? Let's suppose a man owns one hundred sheep, and one of them goes astray. Doesn't he leave the ninety-nine sheep in the wilderness? Doesn't he go after the one that went astray?
What I am actually getting at in regard to updating about it is genuine. If he happens to find that sheep, he is happier with it than the 99 that did not stray.
And so your heavenly Father. For if anyone wrongs you, do not become his transgressor, also not having the Spirit of love any longer, building upon that.
If your brother offends you, go to him alone and tell him his sin is between him and you. If he listens to you, you have gained your brother.
But if he does not listen to you, then take with you one or two more men, so that every matter may be established by the mouth of two or three witnesses. 19:15
But what if he also refuses to hear witnesses? Let him inform the Church about that. And what if he refuses to listen even to the Church? Then you shall consider him no longer as your brother, but you shall treat him as you would treat a worldly sinner or a tax collector.
What I am not going to do is get into the details, but it is a fact, it is accurate: what you close on earth is closed in heaven; and what you open on earth is opened in heaven.
And I have repeated to you the saying: If two of you agree on earth in any matter they seek, my Father who is in heaven will give them that.
Where two or three are gathered in my name, there am I among them. The servant who did not have mercy.
Peter came to Jesus and said, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The Kingdom of the Heavens is like a kingdom of a king who wanted to hold his slaves accountable for the money that he had with them.
And the king began to do that, when a man was brought to him who owed him millions of dollars.
The man could not pay. His master ordered him to be sold, along with his wife, his children, and all his possessions, until he repaid his debt.
The slave fell to his knees before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will pay you everything."
His master had mercy on him, so he forgave his debt, pardoned him, and set him free.
But that servant went out and found one of the servants who owed him some dollars. He seized him and began to strangle him, saying, "Pay back what you owe me!"
The other server fell to his knees. "Give me a break," he begged. "I'll pay you back."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and had the man thrown into prison. There he would stay until he paid back what he owed.
The other servants saw what happened and were very worried about it. So they went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then the Lord called his first servant to him and said to him, O my evil servant, I have forgiven you all your debt because you requested it from me.
Did you not want to be in his favor, having an advantage over the enemies, just as you saved me from the enemy?
His master being angry with him, he delivered him to the jailers. He will be punished until he pays all his debt.
Thus will my heavenly Father do unto you, if ye forgive not every one his brother their trespasses.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea beyond the Jordan.
Large crowds followed him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to him to test him, and they asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any and every reason?"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read that from the beginning the Creator made them male and female?" (Genesis 1:27)
Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife; and they shall be one flesh. (Genesis 2:24)
They are not two, but one. Therefore, what God has joined together, let no one separate."
They said: "Why then did He command Moses that a man should let his wife go with a writing, and he should bring a witness?"
Jesus answered, "Moses permitted you to divorce your wives because of the hardness of your hearts. But it was not like this from the beginning."
This is what I am telling you. Whoever divorces his wife and marries another is an adulterer. A man may divorce his wife if she has violated the marital covenant with him.
The disciples said to him, "If this is the case between a husband and his wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, 'Not everyone can accept this word, but only those for whom it has been prepared by God. For there are eunuchs who were born that way, and there are eunuchs who were made that way by others, and there are eunuchs who deliberately choose to live like eunuchs for the sake of the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever can accept this should accept it for the sake of my name.'
Some men cannot have children because they were born that way. And some, people made them that way. And others, they made themselves that way to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever can accept living like this, let them do so." Little children are brought forward to Jesus.
Some people brought little children to Jesus. They wanted him to place his hands on them and pray for them. But the disciples prevented the people.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them, and he departed from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man.
A man said to him, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to inherit eternal life?"
« Why do you ask me about what is good? » Jesus answered. « What is good is one. But it is for whoever wants to enter into life eternal, to observe the commandments ».
"Which?" the man asked. Jesus said, "You shall not murder. You shall not commit adultery. You shall not steal. You shall not bear false witness."
Honor your father and your mother (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) and "love your neighbor as yourself" (Leviticus 19:18).
I have translated all those commandments, said the young man. So what else am I supposed to do?
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
Upon hearing that, the young man left the place sad. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "Truly I say to you, it is difficult for those who are wealthy to enter the kingdom of God."
And I tell you again, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle, than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God.
When the disciples heard this, they were astonished and said, "Then who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "This is impossible for mortals, but for God all things are possible."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything and followed you! What then will be ours?"
Jesus said to them: "Truly I tell you, that in the new world, the Son of Man will sit on the throne of his glory, and you, who have followed me, will sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel."
Whoever leaves a house or family or money, it is because of me. They will inherit a hundredfold. And they will obtain everlasting life.
But many of the first will be last; and many of the last will be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The Kingdom of the Heavens is like a man, a vineyard owner, who dug a vineyard, put a hedge around it, and built a watchtower. Then
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
At about nine o'clock in the morning he went out again. He saw others standing in the market place doing nothing.
He said to them, "You also, go and work in my vineyard. I will pay you the right wage."
So they went. He also went out towards the middle of the day, around the third hour, and he did the same.
About the fifth hour he went out and found others standing. He asked them, 'Why do you stand here idle all day long?'
They said, "Because no one has appointed us." But he said to them, "Go, you also, and work in my vineyard."
And when evening came, the owner of the vineyard said to the manager in charge of the workers: "Call the workers and pay them their wages, beginning with the last ones who were hired first, and then go to the first ones."
The workers who were hired around five o'clock arrived. Each of them received a full day's wage as usual.
When the first group's workers came, he hired them for a fair wage. He gave each of them their day's wage, and they went away happy.
Upon receiving it, they began complaining about the landlord.
They said, "These people who were recently hired have only worked for one hour, yet you have paid them the same as you pay us. We are the ones who have done most of the work, and we have spent the whole day under the scorching sun."
The employer answered them and said to one of them: "My friend, I am dealing with you fairly, aren't I? You agreed to work for your usual daily wage, didn't you?"
Go and get your money. I want to give those I recently hired the same salary I gave you.
Aren't I the owner of my money and I do with it whatever I want? Do you feel that you are betrayed because I gave generously to others?
The first are last and the last are first.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. And on the way, He took the twelve aside to speak to them.
He said, "We went up to Jerusalem, because the Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the scribes. And they will condemn him to death."
Then they hand him over to the pagans, who mock him, flog him, crucify him, and kill him on the third day.
The mother of my sons Zebedee came to Jesus, and her two sons followed her. She fell down at his knees and asked him for a request.
"What do you want?" Jesus asked her. "Give me this cup," she said, "that this one sitting at your right hand may sit down." And the other one at his left hand said, "Lord, I pray
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup that I am to drink?" They said, "Yes, we can."
Jesus said to them: "You will all drink from my cup. As for the seat at my right hand and at my left hand, it is not for me to give them, for they are prepared for those who are ready for them."
The other ten pupils had heard about this; they were angry with the two brothers.
Jesus gathered them and said, "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over their people, and their great men exercise authority over them. The rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and those who are great exercise authority over them.
Do not be like that; but whoever wants to become great among you must be your servant, and whoever wants to be first must be your slave, just as the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many.
And whoever wants primacy, let him be your servant.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give his life as a ransom for many, who are being freed from it.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A great crowd followed him.
She is the twenty-seventh, slim, tall, with a straight nose, light brown eyes, and a small mouth. She is also intelligent, kind, and a hard worker.
They said to the crowd, "The plan is that if they hold back, we will disperse." And they ordered them to be silent. Their voices rose even more, saying, "O Lord, O son of David, have mercy on us!"
And Jesus stood up and called them and said, "What do you want me to do for you?"
Our lord, we hope to see.
Jesus felt deep compassion for them. He touched their eyes. Sight returned to them immediately. And they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
And after they got near to Jerusalem, and arrived at the village of Bethphage, which was on the Mount of Olives, Jesus sent two disciples, saying to them, "Go into the village which is opposite you, and as soon as you have entered into it, you will find a young ass, which no one has ever yet mounted; untie it and bring it to me."
He said to them, "Go to the village before you. And as soon as you enter it, you will find a donkey's foal tied there. Its mother will be with it. Untie them and bring them to me."
If anyone tells you anything, say that the master needs them. The owner will send them immediately.
This was done so that what was spoken by the prophet might be fulfilled.
Say to the daughter of Zion, "Behold, your king is coming to you, gentle, and riding on a donkey, on a colt, the daughter of a donkey." (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had instructed them to do.
They brought a donkey and a goat, put their coats on them, then Jesus sat on the coats.
A very large crowd spread their coats on the road. Others cut the branches of trees and spread them on the road.
Some people spread their cloaks on the road, and others spread leafy branches from the trees. The crowds who went ahead of him and those who followed kept shouting: "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up. And the people asked, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, "This is Jesus, the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus Expels the Sellers from the Temple
Jesus entered the temple and began to drive out all those who were selling and buying in it. He overturned the tables of the moneychangers and the chairs of those who sold doves.
He said to them, "It is written, 'My house shall be called a house of prayer for all peoples' (Isaiah 56:7). 'You have made it a den of robbers'" (Jeremiah 7:11).
They brought him those who were blind and lame to the temple, and he healed them there.
And the chief priests and the scribes of the law saw the wonderful things that he did, and they heard the children also in the temple crying and saying, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" And when they saw it, they were very angry.
Do you hear what these children are saying? asked his disciples. And Jesus answered, Yes. Have you never read what is written? It is written: 'From the lips of children and infants, you have established perfect praise.' (Psalm 8:2)
Then Jesus left the crowd and went away from there to the house. And a great crowd followed him, and Mary, the mother
Early in the morning, Jesus was going back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree on the road. He turned to it and did not find anything in it except leaves. And he said to it: "Let nothing of the fruit ever return in you!" So the tree withered like ashes and became level.
And the disciples were struck with wonder, saying, "How did this fig tree become withered so quickly?"
Jesus answered, "Truly, I tell you, if you have faith and do not doubt, you will not only do what has been done to the fig tree, but even if you tell this mountain, 'Go, throw yourself into the sea,' it will be done.
If you believe, God will answer your prayers. Defining the authority of Jesus.
Jesus entered the temple courtyard. As he taught there, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him and said, "By what authority are you doing these things? Who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered, "I also will ask you one question. If you tell me the answer, then I will tell you by what authority I do these works.
Where did the source of John's baptism come from? From heaven or from men? They discussed this matter among themselves and said, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say to us, 'Why then did you not believe him?'"
But what if we say: "Of the men"? We are afraid of people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet.
They answered him, "We do not know." Jesus said to them, "Neither will I tell you by what authority I do these things."
What do you think about this? A man had two sons. He went to the first son and said, "My son, go and work in the vineyard today."
"I'm not," the son answered. But he changed his mind afterwards and went.
Then the father went to the other son and said the same thing to him, and the son replied: "I will, Father." But he did not.
Which of the two sons did the will of his father? They answered, The first. Jesus said to them, Truly I say to you, that the tax collectors and the prostitutes go into the kingdom of God before you.
John came to show you the right way to live, and you didn't believe him. But tax collectors and prostitutes did believe him. You saw it with your own eyes, but even then you didn't stop your sins and you didn't believe him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. A man owned a piece of land, so he planted a vineyard in it. And he surrounded it with a fence. And he dug a press basin in it. And he built a watchtower. Then he rented the farm to some farmers and left on a journey.
And when the time of harvest came, he sent his servants to the tenants. And he commanded them to gather his portion of the fruit.
But the tenants seized his slaves. They beat one of them. And they killed another. And they threw the third to the ground with stones until he died.
The man also sent his servants to the farmers, another larger group than the first. The farmers treated them in the same way.
Finally, he sent them his son. He said, "They will respect my son."
But the tenants saw the son coming. And they said to each other, "This is the one who will inherit all the master's possessions someday. Let's kill him; then all the property will be ours."
So they seized him, took him out of the vineyard, and slaughtered him.
When the owner of the vineyard returns, what will he do to those tenants?
Those evil people will be destroyed, they answered. "Then the vineyard will be let to others who will render him the fruits of it in the seasons of the harvest."
Jesus said to them, "Have you not read what was written: 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner. This was in the Lord's doing, and is wonderful in our eyes'?" (Psalm 118: 22, 23)
Therefore I tell you, the kingdom of God will be taken away from you and given to a nation that will produce its fruit.
Everyone who falls on that stone will be shattered. But the stone will crush whoever falls on it.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. They realized he was speaking about them.
So they looked for a way to arrest him, but they were afraid of the crowd, because the people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus said to them again in parables: "The kingdom of heaven is like a king who owned a vineyard,
This is what the kingdom of heaven is like. A king prepared a wedding feast for his son.
He sent his servants to those invited to the dinner. The servants told them to come. But they refused.
Then he also sent other messengers, saying, "Go, tell those who have been invited, 'Behold, I have prepared my dinner; I have killed my oxen and my fatlings, all of them; and everything is ready. Come to the marriage feast.' " Do you hear that?
But the people did not listen. So one went to his vineyard and another to his business.
The rest of the people seized his servants. They treated them badly and then killed them.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to eliminate them. So they killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to the servants, "The wedding dinner is ready for the newlyweds. But the invited guests whom I invited were not worthy to attend."
Go into the street corners. Invite everyone you find to dinner.
The servants went out into the streets and gathered everyone they found, those with good and bad faces. And it was only moments before the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. He noticed a man who was not wearing the wedding clothes.
My friend, how did you get in here without your wedding clothes? The man could not find anything to say.
Then the king said to the servants, "Tie his hands and feet. Throw him into the outer darkness. There people will weep and grind their teeth."
«Many are called, but few are chosen.» Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and began to plot how they might entangle Jesus in His words.
They sent their disciples to him. They sent the Herodians with them. And they said, "Teacher, we know that you are a truthful man, and you teach the way of God in truth, and you are not concerned about anyone, nor do you pay attention to the prominence of anyone."
Tell us then, what do you think? Is it right that we pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus perceived their wickedness, and said, Why do ye speak things which are not so?
"Show me the coin that people use to pay taxes." So they brought him a silver coin.
He asked them: "Whose picture is this? And whose words are these?"
They said, "To Caesar." And he said to them, "Give to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's."
When they heard this, they were amazed and left. The marriage, when the dead rise.
On the same day, the Pharisees came to Jesus with a question, because they did not believe in the resurrection from the dead.
They said, "O Moses, this man has been given a great fortune from the bounty of God, and the favor, and if God had willed, He could have hastened for you the punishment. He
There were seven of us brothers. The eldest got married and died. Since he didn't have any children, he bequeathed his wife to her brother.
The same thing happened with the second brother, the third brother, and so on, until the seventh brother.
The woman finally died.
Who would his wife be then? She was married to all of them in the past."
Jesus answered, "You have sinned because you are ignorant of the Scriptures and do not know the power of God."
For as the dead are raised, neither can they be married, nor can their parents give them away in marriage. For they can neither die anymore, nor can they get married, for they are like the angels and are children of God.
What about the dead who rise from their graves? Have you not read what God has said to you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) He is not the God of the dead, but of the living.
