He said: "The end of times is near, and the Kingdom of God has drawn near. Therefore, repent and believe in the Gospel."
One day Jesus was walking along the shore of the Sea of Galilee. And there he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Come, follow me, said Jesus. "I will make you fishers of men."
So they left the fish and followed him for a time.
Then Jesus walked a little further. And when he looked to the right, he saw James son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in the boat repairing the nets.
And after the Master called them immediately, they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men, and they followed Jesus. The Expulsion of Jesus from a Vicious Spirit
And Jesus went away with his disciples to their town, Capernaum. And he entered the synagogue on the Sabbath day and began to teach.
And the people were astonished at his teaching, for he taught them as one having authority. And he was not teaching like the scribes and Pharisees.
And at that very moment, a man in the synagogue who had an unclean spirit cried out.
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One of God!
"Be quiet!" said Jesus firmly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him, screaming.
And everyone was astonished, and they said to one another, "What is this speech? For with authority and power He teaches! Even the spirits of uncleanness obey Him."
News of Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus healed many people.
Jesus went out from the synagogue, and he was followed by a great crowd. So they came to the house of Simon and Andrew, with James and John. Simon's mother-in-law was there, and she was suffering. So they told her, "Jesus is here." They went and woke him up, and they said to him, "Master, your mother-in-law is suffering." He touched her hand, and the fever left her. She got up immediately and waited on them.
Simon's mother-in-law was sick in bed. They told Jesus about her.
So he went to her, took her hand, and helped her rise. The redness left her. She hastened to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, all who were sick and those possessed came and brought their sick and those who were possessed to Jesus.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them, who were sick with various diseases. He also drove out many demons, but He did not allow the demons to speak because they knew who He was. Jesus' prayer in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning, and the darkness was still hovering around. Jesus got up, left the house, and went to a place where he could be alone. And there he prayed.
Simon and his companions went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they shouted: "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to another place, so I want to travel around in the nearby cities too, because for this purpose I have come out."
He went throughout all of Galilee, teaching in their synagogues and casting out demons. He healed many who were sick with various diseases; among them was a man who had leprosy.
A man who had an unclean spirit came to Jesus, fell on his knees before Him, and said, "If You are willing, You can make me clean."
Jesus was filled with compassion. He reached out his hand and touched the man, saying, "I want to do this. Be cleansed!"
And now he has recovered from his illness immediately.
Jesus immediately banished him and sternly warned the man.
He said, "Don't tell anyone about this." Then he said, "Go and present yourself to the priest and offer the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will serve as proof to the priest and to the people that you are 'pure.'"
But the man went out and began to talk freely, and spread the news to everyone. So Jesus was no longer able to enter a city publicly. He remained outside in desolate places. But people were coming to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
After a few days, Jesus entered once more into Capernaum. The people had heard that he was at home.
Many people gathered until there was no more room. There was no more space even outside the door. So Jesus was speaking to them the Word of God.
Four people brought a man who cannot walk, carrying him.
But they could not come near him because of the crowd. So they went up to the roof of the house where Jesus was and made an opening in the tiles above the place where Jesus was. And when they had made an opening, they let down the bed on which the paralyzed man was lying.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven you."
Some of the scribes were sitting there, reasoning.
Why does this man speak like this? He is saying something very evil! Forgiveness of sins is only for God
Jesus immediately knew what they were thinking in their hearts. He said to them, "What are you thinking in your hearts?"
Is it a sign of being told to this man: are your sins forgiven? Or is it said: get up and carry your bed and walk?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus said to the paralytic:
He said to them, "Rise, pick up your bed and go to your home."
And he rose up and chose for himself a great procession, and then he went onward with them following him in amazement. And the multitude was astonished. And they spoke, glorifying God, and they said, "We never saw anything like this!" And Jesus called for the one who was called the tax-gatherer to come to him.
And once again Jesus went out to the shore of the Sea of Galilee; and a great crowd gathered around him, and he taught them.
And he was walking, when at that time he met Jairus son of Halphas. And Jairus was sitting at the tax office. And Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And he rose and followed him.
After that, Jesus was reclining at the table in the house of Levi. And there, with the disciples, many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him, for there were many who followed him.
There he found some of the scribes, who were Pharisees. When they saw that he ate with sinners and tax collectors, they said to his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard this and said to them: *"It is not those who are well who need a doctor, but those who are sick. I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance."*
The disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting. Some people came to Jesus and said to him, "The disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees also fast. But your disciples are not fasting."
Jesus answered, "How can the pigeon be contented and rejoice and be glad when it is with them? The bridegroom's friends cannot mourn, but his joy is for me."
But death will come upon them, cutting off the way. That is the Day when they will not be able to eat any food.
People do not sew a new piece of cloth onto worn-out clothes, because the new piece would be torn from the old one and further spoil the hole.
People do not pour new wine into old wineskins. For the new wine would burst the wineskins, spilling the wine and ruining the wineskins. Rather, they pour new wine into new wineskins.» Jesus, Lord of the Sabbath Day.
On the Sabbath, Jesus was walking through the field with his disciples. And while they were picking out the grain, the disciples were picking the heads of grain and eating them.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Behold, I do not even speak of doing good on the Sabbath, when one is not even permitted to walk, when the law does not even permit one to rescue a beast of burden from falling into an abyss!"
He answered them, "Have you never read what David did? He and his men were hungry, in need of food."
This was in the time of the high priest Abiathar. David entered the house of God and ate the holy bread, which was only permitted to be eaten by the priests. David also gave some of it to his men.
Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for humankind, and not humankind for the Sabbath.
So the son of man is Lord even of the sabbath.
Healing on Saturday.
Jesus entered the synagogue again, and a man was there whose hand was withered and deformed.
Some of the Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They watched him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath day.
Jesus spoke to the man with the withered and crooked hand and said to him, "Stand up in front of everyone."
Jesus said to them, "What is the truth about the good deed that you have done for the sabbath? Is it lawful on the sabbath to do good or to do evil? To save a life or to kill?" They remained silent.
Jesus looked around him with anger, for he was very moved with pity at their hardness of heart. Then he said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out, and his hand was restored as sound as the other.
Then the Pharisees went away and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, to kill him. And the crowd followed him.
Jesus set out with his disciples to the Sea of Galilee, and a great multitude from Galilee followed him.
The people heard about all that Jesus was doing, and many people came to him. They came from Judea and Jerusalem and from Idumea and from the region east of the Jordan, and also from around Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus asked his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This was to keep the crowd from him.
Jesus healed many people, so the sick were pushing to touch him.
And when the people who are inhabited by the evil spirits saw him, they fell to the ground before him, and the spirits cried out, saying, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus instructed them, saying, "Do not tell this to anyone, saying, 'The Son of Man must suffer many things and be rejected.'"
Jesus went up on a mountain and called to him people, so they came to him.
From among them, he appointed twelve whom he named apostles, and from that day on, they kept him company, and he also sent them out to preach.
They have authority to drive out demons.
Jesus chose the twelve disciples. One of them was Simon, whom he named Peter.
There was James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. Jesus named them "Sons of Thunder". "Boanerges" means "Sons of Thunder".
And there were also Andrew, and Philip, and Bartholomew, and Matthew, and Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And Peter, and Matthias, and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was also one of them. He was the one who would betray Jesus into the hands of his enemies later on. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered a house. And the crowd gathered again, so that Jesus and His disciples were not able to eat because of the crowding.
His family heard about it. So they came to take him. They said, "He is crazy."
Some of the scribes there, who had come from Jerusalem, said, "By the prince of the demons he casts out demons."
Jesus called his disciples and said to them in parables: How can a demon cast out a demon?
If the kingdom fights itself from within, it will not survive.
If the family splits, it will not hold.
